## ⏱️ CHECKPOINTS — read this before waiting on anything

The failure pattern to break: wait out a full validation window, discover a
defect at the end, fix it, start the clock over. That has now happened four
times since May. **Every checkpoint below fires WELL BEFORE 50 settled bets and
each one can independently say "stop, this is wrong."** They are not milestones
on the way to the answer — they are early aborts.

| when | check | what failure looks like | if it fails |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Next firing window** (14:00 / 15:30 / 16:30 / 18:30 UTC) | Actions log for `🎯 V5.31 NBM anchor:` lines | shifts scattered around zero instead of clustering positive | the "NBM runs cold" premise is wrong. Do NOT wait 7 weeks. Lower `NBM_CONSENSUS_ANCHOR` or reconsider the whole diagnosis. |
| **~1 week** | re-run bracket_mid − consensus | still near −1.48, hasn't moved toward 0 | the anchor is not reaching bet selection — a plumbing bug, not a modelling question. Find it before collecting more rows. |
| **~10 days** | `nbm_p50` vs actual (query below) | `nbm_err` near zero while `consensus_err` is worse | anchoring toward consensus is pulling toward the WEAKER estimate. Lower the anchor or revert to 0.0. |
| **~2 weeks** | `V531_` row count and price distribution | far fewer picks than V530_, or all clustered ≥90¢ | Gate 1 or the price floor is now rejecting everything. A model that never bets cannot be validated. |
| **3–7 weeks** | 50 settled `V531_` bets, per-tag | — | only this answers whether it HELPED. |

The 10-day query:

```sql
select city,
       count(*) as n,
       round(avg(actual::numeric - nbm_p50::numeric), 2)     as nbm_err,
       round(avg(actual::numeric - consensus::numeric), 2)   as consensus_err
from settlements
where actual is not null and nbm_p50 is not null
group by city order by nbm_err desc;
```

Positive `nbm_err` = NBM ran cold = V5.31 was right.

**Meanwhile the manual track runs independently and needs no model validation.**
Waiting on the model is not a reason to do nothing for seven weeks.

